# Hand-off: taking ownership of your stack

When the engagement with your operator ends -- whether you are
moving to a self-managed setup, switching operators, or just want
to know you could -- this page walks you through claiming
ownership of every external account, bucket, and credential the
stack depends on. After hand-off you can run, restore, and
migrate the stack without your previous operator's involvement.

This pairs with the contract's "no lock-in" clause. If anything on
this page does not match what your operator handed you, follow up
with them before signing off on the hand-off.

## What lives where

Your stack depends on five external services. Each is a separate
account that you must own (not your operator) by the end of
hand-off:

| Service | What it does for you | Cost |
|---|---|---|
| **Cloudflare** | DNS for your zone + the tunnel that exposes your apps publicly | free for the tunnel + standard DNS; Pro plan optional |
| **Tailscale** | Private admin tunnel between you and the VPS (no public SSH) | free up to 100 devices |
| **VPS provider** (OVH / Hetzner / DigitalOcean / etc.) | The server itself | $5-50/mo depending on shape |
| **S3-compatible storage** (Backblaze B2 / AWS S3 / Wasabi) | Off-site backup bucket | ~$0.005/GB/mo |
| **SMTP provider** (Resend / Brevo / SES / Mailgun) | Outbound email for password resets, invites, MFA | free tier covers small clients |

Two are optional depending on your stack:

| Service | When | Cost |
|---|---|---|
| **AI provider** (OpenAI / Anthropic / Mistral) | If you use the n8n AI nodes or the workflow generator | pay-as-you-go |
| **Second backup provider** | Recommended for disaster prevention -- see [Disaster prevention](disaster-prevention.md) | same as primary backup |

## Hand-off checklist

Walk this with your operator on a video call or in person; do not
do it asynchronously. Each step ends with you confirming an
action; "I see it works" is the gate for moving on.

### 1. Cloudflare account

You should already have a Cloudflare account in your name with
your zone (`{{ cloudflare_zone }}`) on it. If your operator owns
the Cloudflare account today, you need to migrate the zone to
yours.

Steps your operator does, with you watching:

1. In the operator's Cloudflare dashboard, your zone -> Overview
   -> **Move zone** (right sidebar). Pick "Move to another
   account."
2. Cloudflare emails the destination account owner (you) a
   confirmation link. Click it.
3. Confirm the zone now appears under your account at
   [dash.cloudflare.com](https://dash.cloudflare.com).
4. **You** generate a fresh API token at
   [dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens](https://dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens)
   with these scopes:
   - Zone -> DNS -> Edit (on `{{ cloudflare_zone }}`)
   - Account -> Cloudflare Tunnel -> Edit (on your account)
5. Hand the token to your operator -- they use it for one final
   converge to swap the old token out of the VPS.
6. After their converge succeeds, **your operator revokes**
   their old token. Confirm with them in writing that they did.

The tunnel itself is bound to a separate **tunnel token** (not
the API token). Your operator already minted that under your
account during the zone move; re-mint it from your dashboard
after the move under Zero Trust -> Networks -> Tunnels -> your
tunnel -> Configure -> Reset.

### 2. Tailscale tenant

Your operator's Tailscale account is what holds the VPS as a
node today. To take ownership:

1. Create your own Tailscale account at
   [login.tailscale.com](https://login.tailscale.com).
2. In Settings -> Users -> Invite, your operator invites you as
   an admin to their tailnet -- OR --, more cleanly: create a
   FRESH tailnet under your account and have your operator
   migrate the VPS to it.
3. The migration step: your operator generates a new OAuth
   client in YOUR tailnet (Settings -> Trust Credentials -> OAuth
   client, scopes: Auth Keys -> Write, tags: tag:vps). They run
   one converge with the new client; the VPS leaves their tailnet
   and joins yours.
4. **Your operator revokes** their old OAuth client in their
   tailnet. Confirm.

After this step, your operator has no admin access to your VPS
unless you explicitly invite them back as a user on your
tailnet. That is the core handover guarantee -- the admin path is
yours to control.

### 3. VPS provider account

Two paths depending on whether your operator's VPS billing was
under their account or yours:

**Path A: VPS already billed to you.** Confirm you can log in to
the VPS provider dashboard, see the running VM, and have a payment
method on file. You are done with this step.

**Path B: Your operator's account holds the VPS.** Your operator
either transfers the VM to your provider account (most providers
support this -- OVH "Project Transfer," Hetzner "VPS Move
Account," DigitalOcean "Team Transfer") OR you provision a new
VPS under your account and migrate the stack to it. The latter is
what your operator would do if you were forced to switch
providers; see the migration runbook in your operator docs.

Either way, by end of step 3, the VPS billing is on your card
and the provider account login is yours.

### 4. S3 backup bucket + access keys

The bucket your operator created for nightly backups should
already be in your name at the S3 provider. To verify:

1. Log in to the S3 provider's dashboard
   (Backblaze B2, AWS, etc.).
2. Confirm the bucket exists under your account, not your
   operator's.
3. Mint a new access key + secret pair scoped to that bucket
   only (B2: Application Keys with `read` + `write` +
   `listFiles`; AWS: an IAM user with `s3:GetObject`,
   `s3:PutObject`, `s3:ListBucket`, `s3:DeleteObject`).
4. Hand the new pair to your operator; they update vault on the
   VPS. Old keys revoked after one nightly run confirms the new
   pair works.
5. **Critical: write down the restic repository encryption
   password separately.** It is in your operator's vault today.
   Without it, the bucket contents are unreadable ciphertext.
   Save it in your password manager BEFORE the next step.

If your stack also has a WORM mirror bucket configured (per
[Disaster prevention](disaster-prevention.md) step 4), repeat
this step for the worm bucket -- it is at a different provider
and has its own access key pair.

### 5. SMTP provider + API key

Your operator wired SMTP for outbound mail (password resets,
invitations, MFA). If they used a personal Resend / Brevo / etc.
account, that key needs to leave the VPS:

1. Sign up for SMTP provider in your name (Resend free tier:
   [resend.com](https://resend.com); Brevo free: [brevo.com](https://brevo.com)).
2. Verify your domain (`{{ cloudflare_zone }}`) by adding the DNS
   records the provider supplies. Most providers have a one-click
   "verify" after the DNS records propagate (~15 min).
3. Mint an API key scoped to "send transactional email."
4. Hand the key to your operator; they update vault + converge.
   Old key revoked after one outbound email confirms the new one
   works (test by triggering a Keycloak password reset on a test
   user).

### 6. AI provider key (if used)

If your stack has n8n with AI nodes or a workflow generator
configured:

1. Sign up for the provider in your name (OpenAI, Anthropic,
   Mistral).
2. Set spending limits on the account so a runaway workflow
   cannot empty your card -- $50/month is a reasonable starting
   cap.
3. Mint an API key.
4. Hand to your operator for vault update + converge.
5. Verify in n8n's UI that an AI-using workflow still runs.

If you are not sure whether AI is wired, ask your operator. If it
is not, skip this step.

### 7. Vault + recovery secrets

Your operator's "vault" is an encrypted file on their controller
that holds every credential listed above. After steps 1-6, that
vault references YOUR keys (not theirs) -- but the vault file
itself still lives on their machine.

Two paths:

**Path A: You take over operations.** Your operator hands you
the vault file + the age private key that decrypts it (via an
encrypted USB stick, or a password-manager attachment). You also
need an Ansible-capable workstation; your operator can walk you
through that setup or recommend a managed service.

**Path B: You stay managed but switch operators.** Your new
operator generates their own age private key. Your previous
operator re-encrypts the vault with the new public key (a one-line
SOPS rekey command), hands the file off, and shreds their copy.

**Either way:** click the **"Export recovery secrets
(encrypted)"** button under Ops at
[actions.{{ cloudflare_zone }}](https://actions.{{ cloudflare_zone }}/)
and save the resulting `.gpg` file with a passphrase you choose.
That gives you a SECOND copy of every secret, encrypted with a
key only you control. Use this as your insurance: the vault file
is the working copy your operator uses; the recovery export is
the breaks-glass copy you keep regardless of operator status.

### 8. Confirm function end-to-end

Before signing off on hand-off, run through these to confirm
nothing broke during the credential rotations:

- [ ] Log in to your apps via SSO at the public URLs. Each app
      loads, you see your user, your data is there.
- [ ] Public URL works in an incognito window (CF tunnel +
      auth-gate functioning).
- [ ] Trigger a password reset on a test Keycloak user; confirm
      the email arrives (SMTP).
- [ ] Click "Trigger backup now" in OliveTin; confirm the
      backup completes against the new bucket creds (Backups
      panel in OliveTin shows last-run timestamp + size).
- [ ] If a WORM mirror is configured: confirm the
      `catena-restic-mirror` healthcheck shows a green ping
      from the most recent Sunday at
      [healthchecks.{{ cloudflare_zone }}](https://healthchecks.{{ cloudflare_zone }}/).
- [ ] If you took the operator role yourself: run
      `./catena validate --limit <your-host>` from your
      controller and see every Vantage green.
- [ ] If you switched to a new operator: have THEM run the
      validate command and send you the output.

### 9. Update payment + ownership records

Make sure the per-account billing addresses, contact emails,
and payment methods are all in your name -- not your previous
operator's:

- Cloudflare: account settings -> Members; remove your
  operator's email.
- Tailscale: Settings -> Users; remove your operator (if they
  were invited).
- VPS provider: account settings; confirm primary contact +
  payment.
- S3 provider: account settings; same.
- SMTP provider: same.
- AI provider (if applicable): same.

This prevents the awkward case where a credit card on file is
your previous operator's -- it works for the first month and
fails the second. Cleanest cutover: card change AT hand-off.

## What if you skip a step

Each unfinished step is a thread your previous operator can pull
on later. The most common ones, ranked by impact:

| Skipped step | Worst case if your operator goes hostile |
|---|---|
| 1 (Cloudflare) | They can disable your tunnel + delete DNS records, taking your apps offline |
| 2 (Tailscale) | They retain admin SSH to the VPS forever |
| 4 (S3 keys) | They can delete every backup snapshot (WORM mirror is the only defense) |
| 7 (vault) | They retain the master key set even after credential rotations |
| 9 (billing) | A failed payment on their card causes service interruption you cannot fix |

A clean hand-off has all nine done. A practical hand-off has 1, 2,
and 7 done at minimum -- the rest can be cleaned up over the
following weeks without operational risk if your relationship is
amicable.

## After hand-off

Bookmark these:

- This page (you came here to do it once; you may want to do
  parts of it again -- staff change, account migration, etc.).
- [Disaster prevention](disaster-prevention.md) -- the quarterly
  checklist that maintains everything you set up here.
- [Disaster recovery](disaster-recovery.md) -- the index of
  "what to do when X breaks."

If you took on operations yourself, the operator-side
documentation lives in your operator's repo handoff (look for an
`internal_docs/operator/` directory in the codebase you received).
That documentation is for the person who runs converges; this
client wiki is for everyone else.
